vendor/tetorica_sega_psg: a Sega PSG (SN76489) sound chip
  Upstream: https://github.com/kyorohiro/tetorica-soundchip-native at the commit above,
            vendor/tetorica_sega_psg (segapsg.h, segapsg.cpp, LICENSE, README.md), Tetorica's
            own code, Copyright (c) 2026 kyorohiro, BSD-3-Clause; its README credits the
            SN76489 documentation on SMS Power!. Unchanged.
            chip_state.h beside it is that repository's vendor/ymfm/chip_state.h, which
            segapsg.h includes: Tetorica's own code under the repository's BSD-3-Clause
            LICENSE, the same text and copyright holder as the LICENSE here. Unchanged.

vendor/yamaha-smaf-player: a player of SMAF ringtone files (.mmf) with its own FM core
  Upstream: https://github.com/akustikrausch/yamaha-smaf-player, commit
            627f7ad8583ce34c5fec0269ef9778167af080d6 (627f7ad, 2026-07-03), src/, by
            Akustikrausch (Andreas Wendorf), Apache License 2.0
            (vendor/yamaha-smaf-player/LICENSE). Unchanged: the nine files of src/.
  Not here: the example, the docs and the build files. No ringtone is here.

Written for Pro Wave, under the Apache License 2.0: shim/pw_chip.h, shim/pw_fm6.cpp,
shim/pw_chips.cpp and shim/pw_smaf.cpp (a C interface; the FM part follows the order of calls and the output
scaling of msfa's SynthUnit), build.rs (builds the C++ with the cc crate, the FM core's scalar path),
src/lib.rs (the Rust declarations and owners of that interface), Cargo.toml, rustfmt.toml
and this file. Pro Wave's instruments around the cores are in
crates/prowave-engine/src/native_inst/ (fm6.rs; chip.rs and the chip_*.rs files).

"DX7" is a name of its owner; Pro Wave's instrument is called FM6 and reads voices in that
instrument's published voice-dump format.
